{"id":"cash-gift-cards-bought","title":"Gift cards and gift certificates the City buys","question":"How much do departments spend on gift cards and gift certificates, from which sellers, and does the purchasing file capture it?","finding":"Purchase orders coded as gifts, gift certificates or gift cards add up to $2.8 million from fiscal 2019 to fiscal 2027 to date, most of it at Public Health ($1.4 million) and Human Services Agency ($951,790); the largest seller is Safeway Inc ($1.8 million). The peak was fiscal 2020 ($1.0 million) and the figure for fiscal 2026 is $92,646: by this measure the spending is small and falling. The measure is incomplete. The Controller’s auditors counted $2,780,149 of gift cards bought by the Human Services Agency alone between August 15, 2017 and March 8, 2021, where the purchasing file’s gift lines for that agency in fiscal 2019 to 2021 come to $936,387; the audit says the cards included “Visa ReliaCards, which are prepaid debit cards”, which these commodity codes would not catch. For Public Health the auditors counted $487,060 over three years against $765,104 in the file, so there the file shows more than the programmes reported.","how":"Lines of the Controller’s Purchasing Commodity Data, fiscal 2019 to fiscal 2027 to date, whose commodity is “Gifts (Incl. Gift Certificates)” or “Cards Greeting and Gift”, or whose agreement title says gift cards. Amounts are the encumbered value of each line, added up by buying department and seller. The auditors’ totals are read from the Controller’s audits of February 2023 (Human Services Agency) and February 2024 (Public Health), whose periods do not match fiscal years exactly.","limits":"The audits give the purposes: incentives for patients to make or keep medical appointments, feeding vulnerable people, and emergency relief in the pandemic. The commodity code is chosen by whoever keys the order: it also covers gifts that are not cards, and it misses cards bought under a food, supplies or “client assistance” code, through a bank, or by a nonprofit that the City then reimburses. A purchase-order line is money set aside, not money paid, and some lines are accounting adjustments. Nobody who received a card is named or counted. What would settle it: each department’s gift-card inventory log (cards bought, issued and on hand by programme), which the Human Services audit says the Controller’s accounting policies require departments to count and match.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"department","t":"Department","type":"text"},{"k":"vendor","t":"Seller","type":"org"},{"k":"bought","t":"Ordered, fiscal 2019–2027","type":"usd"},{"k":"early","t":"Fiscal 2019–2021","type":"usd"},{"k":"late","t":"Fiscal 2022–2027","type":"usd"},{"k":"peakYear","t":"Largest year","type":"year"},{"k":"peak","t":"Amount that year","type":"usd"},{"k":"described","t":"Described as","type":"text"}],"rows":[{"department":"Public Health","bought":1075748,"early":555779,"late":519969,"peakYear":"2020","peak":258509,"described":"DPH-CA GIFT CARDS","vendor":"Safeway Inc","vendorKey":null},{"department":"Human Services Agency","bought":607736,"early":607736,"late":0,"peakYear":"2020","peak":604390,"described":"gifts, including gift certificates","vendor":"Safeway Inc","vendorKey":null},{"department":"Human Services Agency","bought":198490,"early":183300,"late":15190,"peakYear":"2019","peak":83300,"described":"greeting and gift cards","vendor":"Target Stores","vendorKey":null},{"department":"Juvenile Probation","bought":161250,"early":16500,"late":144750,"peakYear":"2024","peak":45250,"described":"gifts, including gift certificates","vendor":"Apex Industrial","vendorKey":null},{"department":"Public Health","bought":129920,"early":106387,"late":23533,"peakYear":"2021","peak":106387,"described":"gifts, including gift certificates","vendor":"T & S Trading & Enterprise Co","vendorKey":null},{"department":"Public Health","bought":127523,"early":100948,"late":26575,"peakYear":"2020","peak":45034,"d